==English==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|-}}, perhaps from {{der|en|fro|lobeor||swindler}},<ref>{{R:Lexico}}</ref> or of Scandinavian origin, compare dialectal Swedish ''lubber''.<ref>{{R:Etymonline}}</ref>

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈlʌbə(ɹ)/}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-lubber.wav|a=Southern England}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|archaic}} A [[clumsy]] or [[lazy]] person.
#: {{syn|en|Thesaurus:unskilled person}}
#* {{RQ:Carlyle Essays|volume=III|page=147|passage=[T]hree of the boys, of whom Mr. Hector was sometimes one, used to come in the morning as his humble attendants, and carry him {{quote-gloss|Johnson}} to school. {{...}} The purfly, sand-blind '''lubber''' and blubber, with his open mouth, and face of bruised honeycomb; yet already dominant, imperial, irresistible!}}
# {{lb|en|nautical}} An inexperienced or novice [[sailor]]; a [[landlubber]].
# {{lb|en|Southern US}} Common name for the {{vern|eastern lubber grasshopper}} ({{taxlink|Romalea microptera|species}}), likely after "a clumsy or lazy person"

